# Example 07: a continuous physical quantity on a surface.
# Coulombic electrostatic potential on SpCas9 (4OO8), which makes the
# positively charged nucleic-acid channel obvious.
#
# Rules: a quantity that diverges about zero gets a diverging palette and a
# symmetric range. Red/white/blue is the field convention for electrostatic
# potential, so we keep it rather than forcing Okabe-Ito; readers expect
# negative red and positive blue here and a novel palette would mislead.
# This is the one sanctioned exception to the house palette.
open 4oo8
delete /D-F
open ../../palettes/lab_colors.cxc
open ../../presets/Base/publication_white.cxc

surface /A
hide /A cartoons
# Do not pass "surfaces #1.1" here. Pseudobond groups take submodel IDs
# ahead of the surface, so the hardcoded number silently colors the wrong
# model and the surface comes out blank. With surfaces omitted, coulombic
# finds the surface belonging to the atoms itself.
coulombic /A palette redblue range -10,10

# Nucleic acids stay opaque and saturated in front of the surface
color /B lab_sgrna
color /C lab_target

view
turn y 30
view pad 0.05

key redblue :-10 :0 :10 pos 0.04,0.3 size 0.03,0.4 fontSize 17 labelSide right/bottom
2dlabels text "Coulombic potential" xpos 0.03 ypos 0.76 size 17 color lab_black bold true
2dlabels text "(kcal/mol e)" xpos 0.03 ypos 0.72 size 17 color lab_black bold true
2dlabels text "SpCas9  4OO8" xpos 0.03 ypos 0.04 size 18 color lab_gray

save electrostatics.png width 3000 supersample 3
